# Confidence Interval Simulation

## Question

How often does a 95% confidence interval contain the true population mean?

## Method

This simulation creates a population of random integer values from 1 to 100. The program calculates the true mean of the population, then repeatedly takes random samples from that population.

For each sample, the program:

1. Calculates the sample mean.
2. Calculates the sample standard deviation.
3. Calculates the standard error.
4. Builds an approximate 95% confidence interval.
5. Checks whether the interval contains the true population mean.

The confidence interval is calculated using:

sample mean ± 1.96 × standard error

where:

standard error = sample standard deviation / square root of sample size

The simulation repeats this process many times and calculates the percentage of intervals that contain the true population mean.

## Result

Most of the confidence intervals contain the true population mean. Since this module uses a 95% confidence interval method, the capture rate should usually be close to 95%, although it will not be exactly 95% every time because the simulation is random.

The program also creates a plot of the simulated confidence intervals.

In the plot:

Green intervals = interval captured the true population mean
Red intervals = interval missed the true population mean
Black dashed vertical line = true population mean

## What I Learned

This simulation demonstrates the meaning of a confidence interval through repeated sampling.

A 95% confidence interval does not mean that one specific interval has a 95% chance of containing the true population mean after the interval is created. Instead, it means that if we repeat the sampling process many times, about 95% of the intervals created by this method should contain the true population mean.

This helped me understand confidence intervals as a long-run repeated sampling idea, not just as a formula.

